\section{Kingdom blanks columns}{

20 of the 32 core columns are conditional on kingdom. On a plant query the 18
abundance columns, \code{numsites_with_yes_record} and
\code{proportion_sites_with_yes_record} are entirely \code{NA}; on an animal query
\code{numindividuals_with_yes_record} and
\code{proportion_individuals_with_yes_record} are. Their types are declared, so an
all-\code{NA} column still comes back numeric rather than being guessed.
}

\section{Seasonal windows}{

This product is \strong{never} windowed, unlike
\code{\link[=npn_export_individual_phenometrics]{npn_export_individual_phenometrics()}} and
\code{\link[=npn_export_site_phenometrics]{npn_export_site_phenometrics()}}. Its buckets anchor to \code{start_date} and
\code{year}, \code{start_date} and \code{end_date} are output columns, so time is already
resolved in the rows. \verb{[start_date, end_date]} means the continuous span it
looks like it means.
}

\section{Large queries}{

The estimate here is arithmetic rather than a count: one row per taxon-grain
value, per phenophase-grain value, per time bucket. Two honest limitations
follow. It \strong{over-estimates} at coarser \code{taxon} grains, where many species
collapse into one row, and it \strong{under-estimates} when \code{pheno_class_ids} or
\code{phenophase_categories} narrow the phenophases without narrowing
\code{phenophase_ids}. It only warns, so neither is worth engineering around;
\code{options(rnpn.preflight = FALSE)} switches it off.
}

